<!doctype html>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1">
<title>{{.Title}}</title>
<link rel="icon" type="image/png" sizes="32x32" href="{{.Favicon}}">
{{/* Served rather than inlined, so a browser fetches it once and is told 304
     for every load after that. It was inlined while `roz render` wrote the
     page to a file, where a linked stylesheet had nowhere to be fetched from;
     nothing writes a file any more. */}}
<link rel="stylesheet" href="{{.StylesheetURL}}">

<div class="wrap">
  <header>
    {{/* The owner is a label from `roz config`, and usually unset. When it
         is, the heading is what it always was rather than a dangling
         separator. */}}
    <h1><a href="/">roz</a>{{with .Owner}} <span class="owner">· {{.}}</span>{{end}}</h1>
    <div class="stamp">{{.GeneratedAt}} · {{.Stamp}}</div>
  </header>

  {{/* Where this page sits. Absent on the index, which is the root: a
       breadcrumb of one crumb pointing at itself is furniture. */}}
  {{if .Crumbs}}
  <nav class="crumbs">
    {{range $i, $c := .Crumbs}}{{if $i}} <span class="sep">›</span> {{end}}{{if $c.Href}}<a href="{{$c.Href}}">{{$c.Label}}</a>{{else}}<span>{{$c.Label}}</span>{{end}}{{end}}
  </nav>
  {{end}}

{{template "body" .}}

  {{template "note" (index .Notes "footer")}}

  <footer>The page is a view; the database is the truth. Nothing reads it back.</footer>
</div>
{{if .Live}}
{{/* The server streams a line on /events whenever the log moves. Reloading
     whole is crude and correct: the page is cheap to build and holds no state
     worth keeping. Comments inside <script> are stripped by html/template,
     which is why this one is out here. */}}
<script>new EventSource("/events").onmessage = () => location.reload();</script>
{{end}}
{{with .Graph}}{{if not .Empty}}
{{/* The one thing on this page fetched from anywhere else, and the only reason
     it is worth it: a layered graph layout with edge routing is a large piece
     of work that is already done well. Pinned to an exact version and checked
     against its hash, so a compromised CDN cannot run script against a page
     that has no authentication and can read the whole queue.

     Fetched only when there is a diagram to draw. A queue with no recorded
     dependencies renders a sentence saying so, and pulling two and a half
     megabytes across the network to draw a sentence is the kind of thing
     nobody notices until they are on a train.

     If it does not load — offline, blocked, hash mismatch — nothing runs and
     the <pre> stays as it was: the diagram written out as text, which is
     legible, and considerably better than an empty box.

     ELK does the layout, and dagre draws it if ELK does not arrive. On the
     current queue ELK crosses five pairs of edges where dagre crosses
     twenty-four — measured by sampling the drawn paths, not by eye — and it is
     the only thing that moved: reordering the source so dagre's own heuristic
     sees each component together took twenty-four to twenty-three.

     Its render chunk is imported dynamically and so carries no integrity hash,
     which the entry point's would not cover anyway. Pinned by version, and a
     loopback tool that already pulls mermaid from the same origin is not made
     meaningfully worse by it -- but it is a weaker promise than the line
     above, and worth knowing.

     htmlLabels is off because labels carry pull-request titles, which come
     from GitHub and are not ours: off makes a label SVG text rather than
     markup. securityLevel is left at its default, which is strict. It used to
     be loose, for `click ... href` statements in the diagram source -- and
     those are gone, because ELK draws an empty diagram when it meets one. The
     navigation is bound to the drawn nodes instead, from a map the server
     writes out.

     htmlLabels is set twice, and both are load-bearing. Setting it only under
     flowchart leaves the renderer reading the top-level default, which is on:
     a label of `<img src=x onerror=…>` then reaches the DOM as an actual img
     element inside a foreignObject. Checked in a browser, not reasoned about —
     with one of them the diagram has no foreignObject at all, and with the
     other it has two. */}}
<script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/mermaid@11.4.1/dist/mermaid.min.js"
        integrity="sha384-rbtjAdnIQE/aQJGEgXrVUlMibdfTSa4PQju4HDhN3sR2PmaKFzhEafuePsl9H/9I"
        crossorigin="anonymous" referrerpolicy="no-referrer"></script>
{{/* Classic and immediate, and it has to be both. mermaid's startOnLoad
     defaults to on, and a module script does not run until after
     DOMContentLoaded -- so mermaid draws every diagram itself, with default
     config and dagre, before the module below gets to say otherwise. The run()
     down there then finds nothing left to process and silently changes
     nothing. Cost me an hour: the config said elk and the picture was dagre. */}}
<script>
  if (window.mermaid) { mermaid.initialize({startOnLoad: false}); }
</script>
<script type="module">
  if (window.mermaid) {
    let layout = "dagre";
    try {
      const elk = await import("https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@mermaid-js/layout-elk@0.1.7/dist/mermaid-layout-elk.esm.min.mjs");
      mermaid.registerLayoutLoaders(elk.default);
      {{/* Registering only names the loader; the algorithm itself is a second,
           much larger, dynamically imported chunk. Without this the first
           render races that download and quietly falls back to dagre -- the
           config still says elk, the diagram is just laid out by something
           else, and only a reload looks right. Awaited here so the run below
           has it. */}}
      await elk.default.find((l) => l.name === "elk").loader();
      layout = "elk";
    } catch (e) {
      {{/* Offline, blocked, or a version that no longer registers: dagre lays
           it out and the diagram is worse rather than absent. */}}
    }
    mermaid.initialize({
      startOnLoad: false,
      htmlLabels: false,
      layout: layout,
      {{/* Smaller than the page, not just smaller-looking. mermaid's default
           is 16px, which drew the diagram larger than the 15px it sits under
           and made the least detailed thing on the page the loudest. Set in
           config rather than the stylesheet because the layout measures the
           text: shrinking it in CSS alone would leave every box sized for
           16px, and here the canvas comes in with it.

           themeVariables, not the top-level fontSize -- that one is accepted,
           changes nothing, and leaves the injected stylesheet saying 16px. */}}
      themeVariables: {fontSize: "9px"},
      {{/* wrappingWidth is in pixels and mermaid's default is 200, which at
           this font size is a very long line: labels stayed on one row and the
           graph grew sideways instead of wrapping. 100 breaks them into two or
           three short rows, which is what makes the whole thing narrow enough
           to mostly fit. padding is the box's own inset, 8 by default -- at 9px
           text that reads as a lot of empty border per node.

           useMaxWidth false so the diagram is drawn at the size it needs and
           scrolls inside its box. The default writes width="100%" onto the
           svg, which scales a wide graph down to the column until the labels
           are unreadable -- and a diagram nobody can read is a diagram nobody
           drew. Still needed: the 9px/100/5 combination gets close to fitting
           the column unaided, but not close enough to hand the scaling back. */}}
      flowchart: {
        htmlLabels: false,
        curve: "basis",
        useMaxWidth: false,
        wrappingWidth: 100,
        padding: 5,
      },
    });
    await mermaid.run({querySelector: "pre.mermaid"});

    {{/* The navigation `click` statements used to carry. A drawn node's id is
         `flowchart-<the diagram's name for it>-<a counter>`, so the counter
         comes off and what is left is what the link map is keyed by. Bound
         after the run, since none of it exists until then. */}}
    const source = document.getElementById("graph-links");
    const links = source ? JSON.parse(source.textContent) : {};
    for (const node of document.querySelectorAll(".graph .node")) {
      const href = links[(node.id || "").replace(/^flowchart-/, "").replace(/-\d+$/, "")];
      if (!href) continue;
      node.classList.add("clickable");
      node.addEventListener("click", () => { window.location.href = href; });
    }
  }
</script>
{{end}}{{end}}


{{define "refs"}}
{{if or .PRs .Issues .Project}}
<div class="refs">
  {{if .Project}}<span class="mono">{{.Project}}</span>{{end}}
  {{range .PRs}}
  <span>
    {{if .URL}}<a href="{{.URL}}" class="mono"{{with .Title}} title="{{.}}"{{end}}>{{.Label}}</a>{{else}}<span class="mono"{{with .Title}} title="{{.}}"{{end}}>{{.Label}}</span>{{end}}
    {{if .Status}}<span class="{{if .Bad}}bad{{else}}state{{end}}">{{.Status}}</span>{{end}}
  </span>
  {{end}}
  {{range .Issues}}<span>{{template "issue" .}}</span>{{end}}
</div>
{{end}}
{{end}}

{{/* A slot's prose, or nothing at all. Absent is the ordinary state — most
     slots are empty on most pages — so this draws no container when there is
     nothing to put in one. */}}
{{define "note"}}
{{/* On .Body, not on the value: a missing key yields a zero struct, and a
     struct is always truthy in a template, so `{{if .}}` would draw an empty
     box for every slot nobody has written to. */}}
{{if .Body}}<section class="note"><div class="prose">{{.Body}}</div>
<p class="noteset">written {{.Set}}</p></section>{{end}}
{{end}}

{{/* The whole thing is one span so the key and its state cannot be split by
     a line break, and the template trims its own edges: the newlines around a
     definition are text, and they were reaching the page as a stray space
     before every separating comma. */}}
{{define "issue" -}}
<span class="issue">{{if .URL}}<a href="{{.URL}}" class="mono"{{with .Title}} title="{{.}}"{{end}}>{{.Label}}</a>{{else}}<span class="mono"{{with .Title}} title="{{.}}"{{end}}>{{.Label}}</span>{{end}}{{if .Status}} <span class="state">({{.Status}})</span>{{end}}</span>
{{- end}}

{{/* The dependency diagram, as mermaid source for the client to draw. Nothing
     here knows how it will be laid out: a layered graph with edge routing is a
     solved problem and not one this repository is going to solve again.

     The <pre> holds text. html/template escapes it as HTML text, the browser
     un-escapes it into the DOM, and mermaid reads it back out of textContent —
     so a title full of angle brackets survives all three and is still a title
     rather than markup. */}}
{{define "graph"}}
{{if .}}
{{if .Empty}}
<p class="empty">nothing open is blocked on anything open</p>
{{else}}
{{/* One box per disjoint pile of work, stacked. The link map is shared: it is
     keyed by the diagram's name for a node and the whole graph is numbered
     once, so the binding below does not care which box a node was drawn in. */}}
{{range .Diagrams}}<div class="graph"><pre class="mermaid">{{.}}</pre></div>
{{end}}<script type="application/json" id="graph-links">{{.Links}}</script>
{{end}}
{{if not .Legend.Empty}}
<div class="legend">
  {{with .Legend.States}}<div class="lg">{{range .}}<span><i class="sw {{.Key}}"></i>{{.Label}}</span>{{end}}</div>{{end}}
  {{with .Legend.Shapes}}<div class="lg">{{range .}}<span><i class="sh {{.Key}}"></i>{{.Label}}</span>{{end}}</div>{{end}}
  {{if .Legend.Ready}}<div class="lg"><span>solid is ready to pick up now; faded is waiting on something in front of it</span></div>{{end}}
  {{with .Legend.Edges}}<div class="lg">{{range .}}<span><i class="ln {{.Key}}"></i>{{.Label}}</span>{{end}}</div>{{end}}
</div>
{{end}}
<div class="coverage">
  <p><b>what this draws</b></p>
  <ul>{{range .Coverage}}<li>{{.}}</li>{{end}}</ul>
  {{if or .OmittedProjects .OmittedActions}}
  {{/* Said out loud, because a diagram implies completeness. Something left
       out for a good reason and something missing through a bug look exactly
       the same on a page that does not count them. */}}
  <p class="left-out">left out, having no dependency either way:
    {{if .OmittedProjects}}{{.OmittedProjects}} open project{{if ne .OmittedProjects 1}}s{{end}}{{end}}
    {{if and .OmittedProjects .OmittedActions}}·{{end}}
    {{if .OmittedActions}}{{.OmittedActions}} open action{{if ne .OmittedActions 1}}s{{end}}{{end}}
  </p>
  {{end}}
</div>
{{end}}
{{end}}

{{/* The queue's key, beneath the list. Only the bands the queue actually used:
     six of them beside a queue using two is a second thing to read before the
     first one makes sense. */}}
{{define "key"}}
{{if .}}
<div class="key"><div class="lg">
  {{range .}}<span><i class="{{.Key}}"></i>{{.Label}}</span>{{end}}
</div></div>
{{end}}
{{end}}
